.SS Abstract
One storage class that C still has and C++ threw away.
.PP
C keeps \f[CR]register\f[R] up to and including C23.
C++11 deprecated it and C++17 removed it: Clang rejects it as an error,
GCC and MSVC warn.
.PP
\f[CR]REGISTER.H\f[R] does two things:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]REGISTER\f[R] is \f[CR]register\f[R] where \f[CR]register\f[R]
still means something (C, and C++ before C++11), and nothing in C++11
and later.
Code written with \f[CR]REGISTER\f[R] compiles cleanly everywhere.
.IP "2." 3
In C++11 and later, plain \f[CR]register\f[R] in existing code is made
to compile quietly: the compiler\(aqs own diagnostic is switched off
where it can be, and \f[CR]register\f[R] is defined as nothing only
where it can not.
.PP
The C++ level is read from \f[CR]_MSVC_LANG\f[R] on MSVC, which keeps
\f[CR]__cplusplus\f[R] at \f[CR]199711L\f[R] unless
\f[CR]/Zc:__cplusplus\f[R] is given.
.PP
Library code should say \f[CR]REGISTER\f[R], never \f[CR]register\f[R]:
then it compiles everywhere, with or without the fix below.
