.PP
A \f[CR]register\f[R] storage class that is valid in every language
mode.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Language
T}@T{
\f[CR]REGISTER\f[R]
T}
_
T{
C, K&R to C23
T}@T{
\f[CR]register\f[R]
T}
T{
C++98, C++03
T}@T{
\f[CR]register\f[R]
T}
T{
C++11 and later
T}@T{
\f[I](nothing)\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Like \f[CR]register\f[R] itself it is only a hint.
C still forbids taking the address of a register variable, so code that
compiles as C also compiles as C++.
.IP
.EX
REGISTER unsigned int   INDEX;
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS register
.IP
.EX
#define register
.EE
.PP
In C++11 and later, existing code that still says \f[CR]register\f[R] is
made to compile without errors or warnings:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Compiler
T}@T{
What \f[CR]REGISTER.H\f[R] does
T}
_
T{
Clang
T}@T{
Ignores \f[CR]\-Wregister\f[R] and \f[CR]\-Wdeprecated\-register\f[R]
T}
T{
GCC 7+
T}@T{
Ignores \f[CR]\-Wregister\f[R]
T}
T{
MSVC 2017+
T}@T{
Disables warning C5033
T}
T{
Anything else
T}@T{
\f[CR]#define register\f[R] (C++17 and later only)
T}
.TE
.PP
The diagnostics stay off for the rest of the translation unit, because a
macro that uses \f[CR]register\f[R] expands later, in the code that
calls it.
.PP
Only the last row touches the keyword itself.
A macro named \f[CR]register\f[R] hides a keyword: Clang warns about it
(\f[CR]\-Wkeyword\-macro\f[R]), the MSVC standard library refuses it
(\f[CR]<xkeycheck.h>\f[R]), and GNU explicit register variables
(\f[CR]register int X asm(\(dqr10\(dq)\f[R]) stop working.
So it is the last resort, not the first.
.PP
C is never touched.
.PP
